    "includes": "30 boundary words (0, 00, 000, 01, 02, 007, single separators, dot and uppercase words, leading/trailing empty words, all-number and no-number dash cases); 20 hand-written adversarial strings (zero families, mixed separators, numbers above 64 bits); three 4000-digit words; 60 fixed-seed strings from a word vocabulary; 30 fixed-seed random-character strings; 8 strings at the legal maximum |s|=10^5 (mixed words, 50000 zeros, 10^5 separators giving 100001 empty words, one 10^5-letter word, all numbers, long numbers, long zero runs, 4000-digit nines).",
    "maxInputLength": 100000,
    "minInputLength": 1,
    "motivation": "The official suite (26 of 43 kept upstream) has no max-length case. test_recipe_600A re-implements mutants modelled on selected WRONG_ANSWER sources (the sources are not executed); each mutant is killed by at least 25 extras: zero rejected by w[0]!=\"0\" (109331195, 105967924, 105789683), isdigit-only acceptance of leading zeros (154914789), int()==0 accepting 00 (111955085), empty/dotted words dropped (121158382), \"\" printed instead of - (105824496), only \"01\" rejected (162712627), \";\" used as joiner (105811063), lines swapped (105963405), lowercase-only classification (151263146), \"0\" accepted only as the last word (173914988).",
    "numberLengthCap": "Digit-only words in the extras are capped at 4000 digits. CPython >=3.11 int() raises ValueError on strings over 4300 digits. The two pinned Python 3 OK sources would therefore fail a statement-legal 10^5-digit number under a modern interpreter: 14513434 calls str(int(i)) without a guard and raises RE, and 14513478 catches the error and misclassifies the word as a non-number. That is an interpreter artifact, not an algorithmic error, so those cases were deliberately not authored. The parent may add one if it uses only C++ AC references.",
